[00:01.000 --> 00:07.000]  We can also spin two 70 millimeter magnets, one here, and one above, rather than just one,
[00:08.000 --> 00:13.000]  we put three phase coils below and three, three phase coils above.
[00:14.000 --> 00:19.000]  Notice they're staggered by 60 degrees to provide a better magnetic field.
[00:20.000 --> 00:24.000]  Turn off the three phase field, and you see they both start spinning.
[00:25.000 --> 00:27.000]  That's pretty cool, isn't it?
[00:31.000 --> 00:32.000]  That's going a little faster.
[00:39.000 --> 00:40.000]  Reach about 15 hertz.
[00:44.000 --> 00:47.000]  We get no certain speed, and the top magnet goes flat.
[00:48.000 --> 00:50.000]  Look at that. The bottom magnet stays down.
[00:56.000 --> 00:58.000]  Now we have two sets of three phase coils.
[00:58.000 --> 01:02.000]  One down here, and one up there staggered by 60 degrees.
[01:03.000 --> 01:08.000]  We have two magnets in the middle, opposite polarity, so they repair one red, one blue, north, south.
[01:09.000 --> 01:11.000]  We turn on the spin motion.
[01:12.000 --> 01:19.000]  They both spin clockwise, because both of these rotating magnetic fields are clockwise, and there's no change of weight on the scale yet.
[01:20.000 --> 01:25.000]  As we go faster, no change of weight on the scale.
[01:26.000 --> 01:30.000]  We get to about 20 hertz. The top magnet starts spinning flat.
[01:31.000 --> 01:37.000]  We change of
[01:56.000 --> 02:01.000]  non-partial multipath, and we start spinning flat in habenernich.
[02:11.000 --> 02:14.000]  You can do that in experiments, but in theory, that's going to be a problem.
